    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/carol-i-of-romania"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carol-i-of-romania.jpg" alt="Carol I of Romania" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/carol-i-of-romania">Carol I of Romania</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carol_I_of_Romania_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown photographer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom grew out of the union of Moldavia and Wallachia. In 1859 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandru-ioan-cuza">Alexandru Ioan Cuza</a> became prince of both principalities under the nominal suzerainty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and in 1862 they were formally united as the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> as its capital. In 1866 a coalition of Conservatives and radical Liberals forced Cuza to abdicate, and the German prince and Prussian officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carol-i-of-romania">Karl of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen</a> was appointed Prince of Romania to secure Prussian backing for unity and independence. He took the Romanian form of his name, Carol.</p><p>Ottoman suzerainty had become a legal fiction: Romania had its own flag and anthem, and from 1867 its own currency. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–1878, known locally as the Romanian War of Independence, the Treaty of Berlin of 1878 recognised Romania's independence and gave it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-dobruja">Northern Dobruja</a>, while it had to surrender southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In March 1881, as an assertion of full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, parliament raised the country to a kingdom, and Carol was crowned king.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The economy was mainly agricultural. In 1919 about 72% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> worked in farming, and the peasantry was among the poorest in the region, with one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s highest birth rates. The prewar Regat was a land of large estates worked by peasants with little or no land. The land reform of 1921 accomplished little: large landowners still held up to 30% of the land, the new plots were too small to feed their owners, and farming collapsed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>.</p><p>Industry grew from mills, brick and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> factories founded before the kingdom; the first oil refinery in the world had been built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ploiesti">Ploiești</a> in 1857, and the CFR railway was founded in 1880. After 1918 the Malaxa company, founded by Nicolae Malaxa in 1921, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> self-sufficient in locomotives by 1930, and the Reșița works added heavy industry. In 1938 Romania produced 6.6 million tons of crude oil and was one of the top oil exporters. The 1930 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 18,057,028 people, 71.9% of them Romanians. Schools followed the French model, but in practice peasants had almost no chance of an education.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bela-kun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bela-kun.jpg" alt="Béla Kun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bela-kun">Béla Kun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bela_Kun_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gyula Jelfy, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> stayed out of the first Balkan war but entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-balkan-war">Second Balkan War</a> in June 1913 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>: 330,000 troops crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, one army occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-dobruja">Southern Dobruja</a> and another threatening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>. In 1916 it declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>. Its offensive into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> was quickly crushed, and by the end of 1916 the Central Powers held Wallachia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> and the oil fields. Despite fierce resistance, notably at the Battle of Mărășești, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s withdrawal left Romania surrounded, and it signed the Armistice of Focșani and in May 1918 the Treaty of Bucharest. It returned to the field on 10 November 1918, and in 1919 its troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> and ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-kun">Béla Kun</a>'s regime.</p><p>Wartime industry turned fortification guns into field and anti-aircraft artillery, and the Danube fleet had four river monitors built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galati">Galați</a>. Between the wars the Industria Aeronautică Română built aircraft such as the IAR 80, and factories produced guns, mortars and machine guns under French, Czechoslovak, German and British licences. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> Romania fought with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> until King Michael's Coup of 1944 brought it over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> ruled by Carol I and his descendants. After King Ferdinand died in 1927, his son Carol was passed over because of earlier marital scandals, and the boy Michael reigned with a regency until Carol returned after three years in exile, with the support of the National Peasants' Party, and proclaimed himself king. Until 1938 governments kept the form, if not always the substance, of liberal constitutional rule. The 1923 constitution let the king dissolve parliament at will, and there were over 25 governments between 1930 and 1940.</p><p>The National Liberal Party grew clientelist and nationalist and lost power in 1927 to the National Peasants' Party. Antisemitic and ultra-nationalist parties gained ground, above all the fascist Iron Guard, which practised assassination: it killed Prime Minister Ion Duca in 1933 and Armand Călinescu in 1939. In February 1938 Carol II set up a royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> under a new constitution, and the Iron Guard leader Corneliu Zelea Codreanu was killed in custody that November. The country was divided into 33 counties, after 1925 into 71, and briefly in 1938 into ten ținuturi under royal residents.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon.jpg" alt="Treaty of Trianon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Signature_de_la_Paix_avec_la_Hongrie,_en_t%C3%AAte_Ben%C3%A1rd_%C3%81gost_hongrois(passant_devant_un_piquet_d%27honneur_%C3%A0_Versailles).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agence Rol, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Squeezed between the Ottoman, Austro-Hungarian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empires</a>, the kingdom looked to the West, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, for its cultural, educational and administrative models. The term Old Kingdom, or Regat, later distinguished its prewar lands of Wallachia, Moldavia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> from Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><p>At the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> assemblies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> voted for union with Romania, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1919">Treaty of Saint-Germain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> and the Treaty of Paris of 1920 assigned most of these lands to it. In 1920 Romania was more than twice its 1914 size. The expansion roused the enmity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Greater Romania held large minorities, above all Hungarians and Germans, who were effectively shut out of politics by a rule that state employees speak Romanian, though they kept their own schools, publications and courts; these rights were not extended to other minorities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in particular. French influence was strongest in the early 1920s, German influence in the 1930s, and the oil industry drew German and Italian interest.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ion-antonescu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ion-antonescu.jpg" alt="Ion Antonescu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ion-antonescu">Ion Antonescu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ion_Antonescu_1941.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Great Union did not survive the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a> of 1939 recorded the Soviet interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, and in 1940, under pressure from Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> ceded most of the lands it had gained after 1918, keeping only the southern halves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. Carol II, increasingly unpopular, was forced to abdicate and named General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ion-antonescu">Ion Antonescu</a> prime minister with full powers. Under Antonescu's military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> the country was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legionary</a> Romania, and King Michael I was a figurehead.</p><p>Romania fought on the side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> until King Michael's Coup against Antonescu in 1944, after which the kingdom became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> again, changed sides to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> and recovered Northern Transylvania. The influence of the Soviet Union and the policies of Communist-dominated coalition governments led to the end of the monarchy: on 30 December 1947 Michael I abdicated, and parliament proclaimed the Romanian People's Republic, a Soviet satellite state.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> was a Hohenzollern monarchy proclaimed in 1881 on the independent union of Moldavia and Wallachia. It gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, slid into royal and then military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, and became a people's republic at the end of 1947.</em></p>

